      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the sale price after the first discount:**\n   The original price of the shirt is $30. The first discount is 20% off.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount amount} = 30 \\times \\frac{20}{100} = 30 \\times 0.20 = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Sale price} = 30 - 6 = 24\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the price after the second discount:**\n   Mary has a coupon that offers an additional 25% off the sale price of $24.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount amount} = 24 \\times \\frac{25}{100} = 24 \\times 0.25 = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Final price} = 24 - 6 = 18\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the single percent discount to achieve the final price:**\n   The final price is $18, and the original price was $30. The single percentage discount needed to achieve this can be calculated as follows:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Percentage discount} = \\left( \\frac{30 - 18}{30} \\right) \\times 100 = \\left( \\frac{12}{30} \\right) \\times 100 = 0.4 \\times 100 = 40\\%\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
