      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the final price of the shirt after applying both successive discounts and then find the single equivalent discount that would achieve the same final price.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the price of the shirt after the first 20% discount.\n\nThe regular price of the shirt is $30. A 20% discount means the shirt is sold at 80% of its regular price.\n\\[\n\\text{Price after first discount} = 30 \\times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \\times 0.80 = 24\n\\]\n\nNext, we apply the second 25% discount to the new price of $24.\n\nA 25% discount means the shirt is sold at 75% of its price after the first discount.\n\\[\n\\text{Price after second discount} = 24 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 24 \\times 0.75 = 18\n\\]\n\nSo, the final price of the shirt is $18.\n\nNow, we need to find the single equivalent discount that would reduce the original price of $30 to $18. Let \\( x \\) be the single equivalent discount rate. The final price is then \\( 30 \\times (1 - x) = 18 \\).\n\nWe solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n30 \\times (1 - x) = 18\n\\]\n\\[\n1 - x = \\frac{18}{30}\n\\]\n\\[\n1 - x = 0.60\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 1 - 0.60\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 0.40\n\\]\n\nThe single equivalent discount rate is 40%. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{40}\n\\]",
